  "em-dash | glossary.ts |  are the old calendars' names for the ten ways a passing stem can meet yours — the same cast of ten relations, rotating with the days, months, and years.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A clash is two schedules booked for the same hour — something has to move. It reads as movement, not misfortune: fair weather for cutting a knot or shifting what's been stuck.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A combine is two people finishing each other's sentences — signs that pull toward each other. Doors in that room tend to open with a push instead of a shove.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A god names the flavor of a period, not an instruction — texture to work with.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A harm is a slow leak rather than a burst pipe. It rewards attention before force — caught early, the erosions stay small.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A luck pillar is a ten-year stretch of prevailing weather — the decade's own pair of characters, read against your chart.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A punishment is a stone in your shoe — small, recurring, quieter once named. Awkward more than harmful; it tends to repeat until it's faced plainly.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | A trine is three signs pulling one direction — a crew that has rowed together for years. Two of the three is a frame waiting on its third; all three is the steadiest shape there is.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Days and seasons carrying them tend to suit you. The reading points them out so you can lean in when they come around — and pace yourself when they don't.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Direct Officer (正官) is doing things properly — kept promises, tidy paperwork, a reputation quietly compounding.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Direct Resource (正印) is being looked after — the friend who brings soup without being asked, room to rest and take things in.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Direct Wealth (正财) is the earned paycheck — value that arrives because you kept showing up.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Each day, month, and year carries a pair of its own: one of ten stems and one of twelve animal signs. A reading is what happens when a passing pair meets your chart — where they pull together, where they grind.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Eating God (食神) is making for the joy of it — cooking for friends rather than for a review.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Every day, month, and year carries one of the twelve animal signs, and your chart holds signs of its own — one in each pillar.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Everything in the system sorts into five elements — Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, Water — and so do you: the stem that stands for you carries one.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Favorable elements are the ones that balance your chart — the fuel it runs best on.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Friend (比肩) is company — peers carrying the same pack as you, more done shoulder to shoulder than alone.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Hurting Officer (伤官) is improving the recipe — bright ideas that chafe inside anyone else's format, the wit that annoys the chef.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Indirect Resource (偏印) is learning by your own strange route — hunches, side reading, answers that arrive in the shower.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Indirect Wealth (偏财) is the lucky find — chances arriving sideways like a good parking spot, best held loosely.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Na Yin is each pillar's 'sound' — an old poetic name for the flavor a stem-and-branch pair carries, like 'Great Forest Wood'.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Neither direction is good or bad — grain is just grain. The reading only tells you which way it runs, and you choose how to work with it.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Read every line as weather, not verdict — what the pattern tends to make cheaper or costlier, with the wheel staying in your hands. The same birth details and date always produce the same words; nothing here is random.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Rob Wealth (劫财) is friendly rivalry — a pace-setter at your elbow, good drive that tends to spend as freely as it earns.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Seven Killings (七杀) is pressure that trains you — the coach calling for the hill one more time, heavy going that builds real strength.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Strong or weak describes how much backing your day-master has — season, ground, and allies — not how capable you are.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | Symbolic stars are named patterns the old calendars watched for — motifs a chart keeps returning to. Seasoning rather than structure, and never a prediction.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | The old calendars mapped how the signs relate — some pairs pull together, others pull apart. When a passing sign meets one of yours, the line names the pattern.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | The old calendars split the day into twelve two-hour blocks, and each block carries one of the twelve animal signs — the rat from 11 pm, the ox from 1 am, and so on around the clock.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | The ten gods (十神) are the old calendars' names for the ten ways a passing stem can meet yours — the same cast of ten relations, rotating with the days, months, and years.",
  "em-dash | glossary.ts | The twelve life stages track where a stem stands in its cycle — first growth through peak to rest and back — the way a day moves through morning, noon, and night.",
